Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) in Java
What is OOP?
Object-Oriented Programming is a programming paradigm that organizes code around objects (which contain data and methods) rather than functions and logic. Java is fundamentally an OOP language.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The Four Pillars of OOP:
1. ENCAPSULATION
Concept
Binding data (variables) and methods (functions) together in a single unit (class), while hiding internal details from outside access.

Key Points:
-Protects data from unauthorized access
-Achieved through access modifiers (private, public, protected)
-Exposes only necessary functionality through public methods

Real-World Example:
Think of a bank account - you can deposit/withdraw money (public methods) but cannot directly change the balance (private data).

Benefits of Encapsulation
✅ Data Protection - Prevents unauthorized access
✅ Flexibility - Can change internal implementation without affecting external code
✅ Maintainability - Code is modular and easier to debug
✅ Validation - Can add validation logic in setters
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. INHERITANCE
Concept
Creating new classes (child/derived) based on existing classes (parent/base), reusing and extending their functionality.

Key Points
-Promotes code reusability
-Establishes "IS-A" relationship
-Java supports single inheritance (one parent) but multiple interface inheritance
-Use extends keyword

Real-World Example
Vehicle → Car → ElectricCar - Each level inherits properties from above and adds its own.

Types of Inheritance in Java

Single Inheritance- 	One parent, one child	✅ Yes
Multi-level Inheritance-	Grandparent → Parent → Child	✅ Yes
Hierarchical Inheritance-  One parent, multiple children	✅ Yes
Multiple	Inheritance-   Multiple parents	❌ No (We cannot implement multiple Inheritance through classes but can  via interfaces)
Hybrid	Inheritance-   Combination of multiple	❌ No (We cannot implement hybrid Inheritance through classes but via interfaces)

Important Keywords:
super keyword:
-Access parent class constructor: super()
-Access parent class method: super.methodName()
-Access parent class variable: super.variableName
protected access modifier:
-Accessible within same package and subclasses (even in different packages)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. POLYMORPHISM
Concept
The ability of an object to take many forms. The same method name can have different implementations based on the object type.

Key Points:
-Compile-time (Static) Polymorphism - Method overloading
-Runtime (Dynamic) Polymorphism - Method overriding
-Allows writing more flexible and reusable code

Real-World Example:
A "play()" method - a piano plays music, a football plays sport, a theater plays drama - same name, different actions.

Types of Polymorphism Summary:
Type	         |      Also Known As	    |         Achieved By	          |        Compile/Runtime   |           	Example
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Compile-time	 |     Static Binding	    |        Method Overloading	    |       Compile time	     |     add(int,int) vs add(double,double)
Runtime        |    Dynamic Binding     |       Method Overriding       |        Runtime	Animal   |      a = new Dog(); a.sound();

Method Overloading vs Overriding
Feature	              |        Overloading	               |           Overriding
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Relationship	        |       Same class	                 |        Inheritance
Method name	          |        Same	                       |         Same
Parameters	          |        Must differ                 |         	Must be same
Return type	          |        Can differ	                 |         Must be same or covariant
Access modifier	      |        Can be different	           |         Cannot be more restrictive
Exception	            |       Can throw different	         |         Cannot throw broader
Binding	              |        Compile-time	               |         Runtime
Keyword	              |        None	                       |         @Override (annotation)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. ABSTRACTION
Concept
Hiding implementation details and showing only essential features to the user. It focuses on WHAT an object does rather than HOW it does it.

Key Points:
-Achieved through abstract classes and interfaces
-Reduces complexity by hiding unnecessary details
-Provides a template/contract for subclasses

Real-World Example:
A car's steering wheel - you know turning it left makes the car go left (WHAT), but you don't need to know the mechanical details (HOW).

Abstract Class vs Interface:
Feature	                   |       Abstract Class	                          |  Interface (Java 8+)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Keyword                  |        	abstract	                            |         interface
  Instantiation            |        	Cannot be instantiated	              |          Cannot be instantiated
  Multiple inheritance     |        	No (single inheritance)	              |          Yes (multiple interfaces)
Instance variables	       |         Yes	                                  |          No (only static final)
Constructors	             |         Yes	                                  |          No
Access modifiers	         |         All (private, protected, public)	      |        Only public (implicitly)
Method implementation	     |       Can have both abstract and concrete	    |      Abstract, default, static methods
When to use	               |       Common base with shared code	            |    Contract/capability definition
Performance	               |       Faster (direct inheritance)	            |      Slightly slower (indirection)
